1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:09,380
N.I.H. Fails to Institute Health A root cause failure analysis to show why the world's largest medical research institution

2
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:17,620
only allocates three point three percent of its budget to testing which of the thousands of known-safe treatments actually

3
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:26,880
work in humans. Oxford tested COVID treatments for five hundred dollars per patient and saved a million lives in one hundred

4
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:35,240
days. The N.I.H. spent one point six billion dollars and completed zero trials in four years.

5
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:43,380
A Tale of Two Trials. The difference between efficient and inefficient trial design isn't theoretical.

6
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:48,740
Your species accidentally ran a controlled experiment on itself during COVID.

7
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:54,140
Two countries. Same disease. One spent like it was trying to cure death.

8
00:00:54,660 --> 00:01:03,120
The other actually did. RECOVERY Trial (U.K. Approach). The British spent twenty million dollars over six months.

9
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:11,120
Enrolled forty-eight thousand patients. Found multiple effective treatments, including dexamethasone, a steroid that had

10
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:20,140
been available since nineteen fifty-seven. It reduced COVID deaths by one-third and saved over one million lives.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,540
Cost per patient: five hundred dollars. RECOVER Initiative (N.I.H.

12
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:32,080
Approach). Nearly six hundred potential treatments for Long COVID have been identified.

13
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:39,880
At RECOVERY trial efficiency (five hundred dollars per patient, five thousand five hundred patients per treatment), one

14
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:45,040
point six six five billion dollars buys six hundred completed trials.

15
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:49,820
The N.I.H. received one point six six five billion dollars.

16
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:59,740
Over four years, they completed zero. Only fifteen percent of the budget went to clinical trials; the rest funded observational

17
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:08,300
studies, data management, and administration. Of those enrolled, roughly eleven percent were in interventional trials that

18
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:15,620
actually tested treatments. The other eighty-nine percent were in observational studies, which is the medical equivalent

19
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:24,100
of taking attendance at a funeral. Cost per interventional patient: five hundred and four thousand, five hundred dollars,

20
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:28,860
the price of a house. Nobody got a house and nobody got cured.

21
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:34,200
They got observed. Expensively. The observation was very thorough.

22
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:38,320
It has been peer-reviewed. The peers also observed the patients.

23
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:45,840
The patients are still sick, but they are the most well-documented sick people in the history of sickness.

24
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:54,160
It gets worse. The treatments RECOVER eventually selected were called "truly absurd" by patient advocates.

25
00:02:54,320 --> 00:03:02,200
Low-dose naltrexone, the number one patient-requested treatment (generic, cheap, already showing results in independent

26
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:08,760
studies), was excluded for over two years before finally being added in round two.

27
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:15,940
David Putrino, director of rehabilitation innovation at Mount Sinai, called the spending "largely wasted".

28
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:21,460
Researchers outside RECOVER had lists of drugs to test. The system ignored them.

29
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:27,200
Six hundred trials were possible with that money. Zero were completed.

30
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:33,430
That is the difference between a system that tests treatments and a system that tests patience.

31
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:41,540
The Translation Crisis: All Theory, No Medicine. Basic research is not the problem.

32
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:48,260
Five decades of N.I.H.-funded science built one of the largest drug candidate pipelines in history.

33
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:53,680
Researchers identified targets, characterized molecules, established safety profiles.

34
00:03:54,080 --> 00:04:02,340
That work was essential, and it succeeded. The problem is what happens after the discoveries are made: almost nothing.

35
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:11,200
The pipeline is full. The exit is closed. COVID made this visible for the first time, but it is the institution's default

36
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,630
setting; the way you don't notice a leaking roof until it rains inside.

37
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:23,560
It has been raining inside for fifty years. The N.I.H. has published several papers on indoor rain.

38
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:28,530
There are over twenty thousand F.D.A.-approved drugs that are known to be safe in humans.

39
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:34,740
Thousands more clinically-tested candidates are gathering dust, including over three thousand marketed drugs that could

40
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:41,860
be repurposed for diseases nobody's tried them on. In Alzheimer's alone, researchers have identified hundreds of safe drugs

41
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:45,740
worth testing, yet less than ten percent are in active trials.

42
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:49,420
And the number of clinical trials available for your grandma in St.

43
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:56,660
Louis? Zero. You have a warehouse of safe molecules that have never been systematically tested for new uses.

44
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,260
The warehouse is full. The testing facility is closed for a team-building retreat.

45
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:09,060
This is not hypothetical. In twenty twenty-three, researchers published case reports of severe long COVID patients experiencing

46
00:05:09,060 --> 00:05:13,960
complete remission within days of receiving monoclonal antibody infusions.

47
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:20,760
Previously bedridden patients returned to normal lives, with remissions sustained over two years.

48
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:28,080
The U.S. government had spent over five billion dollars purchasing millions of doses of these same antibodies for acute

49
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:35,660
COVID. When Omicron rendered them ineffective for acute treatment, millions of unused doses sat in refrigerated storage.

50
00:05:36,340 --> 00:05:40,180
Long COVID patients, having read the case reports, begged for access.

51
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:45,140
They organized petitions. They cited the remissions. The government said no.

52
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:52,180
The doses were authorized for acute COVID only, and the authorization had been revoked because they didn't work for acute

53
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:56,500
COVID, which meant they could not be given to anyone, for anything, ever.

54
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:03,300
The antibodies matched the original variants; the long COVID patients had been infected by the original variants.

55
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:09,100
Nobody tested this. Nobody ran trials. Nobody repurposed the stockpile.

56
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:17,820
The first formal Phase two trial of monoclonal antibodies for long COVID is scheduled for mid-twenty twenty-six, four years

57
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:21,940
after the doses were available and several years after they expired.

58
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:27,880
Five billion dollars of medicine. Millions of doses. Patients who wanted them.

59
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:35,200
Case reports showing they worked. The doses expired. The patients didn't; they just kept being sick.

60
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:44,160
Your government threw away the cure and kept the disease. On Wishonia, we have a word for this, but it is a word I am not

61
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:51,620
permitted to use in your language because it is considered a slur against the competence of your entire species, so I will

62
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:56,820
simply note that the word exists and move on. Then there's metformin.

63
00:06:56,880 --> 00:07:05,240
A phase three randomized controlled trial, or R.C.T. (COVID-OUT) showed it reduces long COVID incidence by forty-one percent.

64
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:10,170
It costs four dollars a month. It has been generic for decades.

65
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:17,040
It is globally available. The N.I.H. guideline response: "insufficient evidence to recommend for or against".

66
00:07:17,960 --> 00:07:22,060
Long COVID costs one hundred and seventy billion dollars a year.

67
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:30,660
The four dollar pill that cuts it by forty-one percent has no corporate sponsor, because nobody profits from a generic.

68
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:38,460
This is the entire chapter in one sentence. To appreciate the scale: long COVID costs the U.S.

69
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:46,580
an estimated one hundred and seventy billion dollars annually in lost wages alone, with roughly seventeen million Americans

70
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:54,440
currently affected. A RECOVERY-style pragmatic trial would have cost approximately twenty-four million dollars and could

71
00:07:54,440 --> 00:08:01,900
have produced results in months. Instead, your government spent five billion dollars buying the medicine and is now paying

72
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:08,260
one hundred and seventy billion dollars a year in economic damage from the disease it didn't bother to treat.

73
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:14,800
The ratio of what you're losing to what the trial would have cost is seven thousand and eighty-three to one.

74
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:21,240
The N.I.H. is, by its own metrics, a publishing house that occasionally dabbles in medicine.

75
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:25,720
It publishes two point five million funded papers annually.

76
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:29,860
It produces approximately fifty new drug approvals annually.

77
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:35,419
That's a conversion rate from paper to patient of zero point zero zero two percent.

78
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:44,780
For every fifty thousand papers, one drug reaches a human. The rest become citations in other papers, which become citations

79
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:49,380
in other papers, in an infinite loop of academics reading each other's homework.

80
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:55,620
On Wishonia, we call this a "knowledge ouroboros." It looks productive from the inside.

81
00:08:55,920 --> 00:09:00,760
From the outside, it's a snake eating its own tail and writing a paper about the nutritional content.

82
00:09:01,660 --> 00:09:08,280
Plenty of Knowledge. No Translation. About five percent of patients have participated in clinical trials.

83
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:12,700
About forty-four point eight percent would participate if invited.

84
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:20,480
About nine to eleven percent have ever been invited. Nearly half of all patients would join a trial if asked.

85
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:28,540
Almost nobody asks. Your species has a room full of volunteers holding their hands up, and a system that looked at the room

86
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:34,020
and decided to study the clipboard instead. The clipboard is very well-studied.

87
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:38,530
It has been studied for decades. Multiple papers have been written about the clipboard.

88
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:43,000
One of them won an award. The volunteers are still holding their hands up.

89
00:09:43,480 --> 00:09:46,560
Some of them have died. This has been noted on the clipboard.

90
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:52,820
Understanding disease and testing treatments in humans are completely different activities.

91
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:59,070
One requires microscopes. The other requires giving people medicine and seeing if they stop dying.

92
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:05,230
In nineteen seventy, spending heavily on microscopes made sense; you didn't know enough to test intelligently.

93
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:13,120
In twenty twenty-five, after two point five million papers a year for five decades, the ratio should have shifted.

94
00:10:13,780 --> 00:10:21,780
It didn't. The N.I.H. chose microscopes in nineteen seventy and has not reconsidered since, which is the institutional equivalent

95
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:25,360
of still crawling because you learned to crawl before you learned to walk.

96
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:34,380
The Allocation Problem. Your National Institutes of Health has the word "Health" in its name and an annual budget of forty-seven

97
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:39,740
billion dollars. Fifty-five million of you die annually from disease.

98
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:43,700
You would think those two facts would be related. They are not.

99
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:51,100
Of two hundred and forty-seven billion dollars the N.I.H. spent over a decade, exactly three point three percent went to

100
00:10:51,140 --> 00:10:55,360
testing which of the thousands of known-safe treatments actually work in humans.

101
00:10:56,120 --> 00:11:02,720
The other ninety-six point seven percent funded understanding disease mechanisms, translating discoveries toward (but never

102
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:10,180
into) clinical use, training scientists, observational programs that spend billions watching disease without treating it,

103
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:17,080
and a side quest engineering bat viruses, which is called "research" the way watching someone drown from a boat is called

104
00:11:17,080 --> 00:11:24,320
"lifeguarding.". Basic research is not the problem. Understanding disease is how you find candidates to test.

105
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:29,860
The problem is that after fifty years and a trillion dollars, the ratio hasn't moved.

106
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:37,360
The marginal value of the eighty-fifth cent on basic research is near zero when you're spending three cents on the only

107
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:41,850
activity that turns research into medicine. You don't have a knowledge deficit.

108
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:49,660
You have an allocation deficit. Three point three percent on trials might be defensible if the testing queue were empty.

109
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:57,560
It isn't. There are roughly nine thousand five hundred compounds with established safety profiles, and ninety-nine point

110
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:02,220
seven percent of their possible disease applications have never been tested.

111
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:09,380
Most are off-patent, which means no pharmaceutical company will fund trials for them, which means the N.I.H.

112
00:12:09,380 --> 00:12:16,700
is the only entity that could, which means it is specifically choosing not to do the one thing only it can do.

113
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:21,460
The pipeline is overflowing with candidates. The bottleneck is trials.

114
00:12:21,980 --> 00:12:28,170
The drugs are sitting there. Safe. Ready. Unpatentable. They've been waiting for decades.

115
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:32,400
Some of them have been waiting longer than most of your scientists have been alive.

116
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:40,520
It's like spending ninety-six point seven percent of your grocery budget on cookbooks and three point three percent on food,

117
00:12:40,620 --> 00:12:47,020
then wondering why you're starving. Except the cookbooks are very prestigious and the starving people aren't on the committee

118
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:53,660
that decides whether to buy more cookbooks. A J.A.M.A. study broke down two hundred and forty-seven point three billion

119
00:12:53,660 --> 00:12:57,380
dollars in N.I.H. spending (twenty ten to twenty nineteen).

120
00:12:58,100 --> 00:13:05,560
I have organized it from "largest amount spent not on clinical trials" to "smallest amount spent not clinical trials," though

121
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:08,940
I should note that none of it is labeled "not clinical trials."

122
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:15,140
It is labeled "investment in the future." The future has been invested in for fifty years.

123
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:22,280
It has not yet arrived. Eighty-four point nine percent, or two hundred and nine point nine billion dollars, is dedicated

124
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:29,940
to basic research for understanding disease mechanisms, molecular biology, and genomics; while essential for identifying

125
00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:36,000
new targets, eighty-five cents of every dollar is a lot of understanding and not much testing.

126
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:44,320
Eleven point eight percent, or twenty-nine point three billion dollars, goes to non-trial applied research that translates

127
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:50,080
discoveries toward practical use, but without the final step of testing them in patients.

128
00:13:51,020 --> 00:13:58,920
The Clinical and Translational Science Awards, or C.T.S.A., infrastructure program spends about one billion dollars per

129
00:13:58,920 --> 00:14:07,440
year in trial infrastructure, coordination, and training. Observational studies for population health data collection (such

130
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:15,680
as large-scale observational cohorts costing two point one six billion dollars) are valuable for identifying patterns, but

131
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:23,840
observation without intervention doesn't cure anyone. Other applied research includes drug characterization, health services

132
00:14:23,840 --> 00:14:33,060
research, and epidemiology. Only three point three percent, or eight point one billion dollars, is spent on phased clinical

133
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:40,200
trials, which is the only category that directly tests whether treatments work in humans through phases one to three.

134
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:47,280
Phase one accounts for one point five billion dollars, or eighteen point five percent of the clinical trial budget.

135
00:14:47,820 --> 00:14:52,440
Phase two accounts for three point five billion dollars, or forty-three point two percent.

136
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:58,260
Phase three accounts for two point six billion dollars, or thirty-two point one percent.

137
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:07,140
The N.I.H. covers only three point seven to four point three percent of Phase three costs, leaving completion to industry,

138
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:10,800
which only funds trials with patent-protected profit potential.

139
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:19,880
Other unclassified spending totals zero point five billion dollars, or six point two percent, for cross-phase overhead,

140
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:23,480
pilot studies, and trials not classified to a single phase.

141
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:31,800
Pragmatic trials (eighty-two times cheaper per patient) are severely underfunded despite proven efficiency.

142
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:37,090
Why This Allocation Exists. This is not the fault of individual researchers.

143
00:15:37,980 --> 00:15:40,840
Most became scientists because they wanted to cure diseases.

144
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:49,050
The system they work in rewards everything except curing diseases, and those rewards were designed by someone else entirely.

145
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:54,220
Huge drug companies with political influence love the N.I.H.

146
00:15:54,360 --> 00:15:58,560
Taxpayers absorb the risk of basic research; industry patents whatever works.

147
00:15:59,080 --> 00:16:04,520
It's like if your neighbor paid for your kitchen renovation, and then you charged them rent to eat there.

148
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:10,180
But independent trials comparing drugs head-to-head? Those are terrifying.

149
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:16,060
They might reveal that the eighty thousand dollar drug works about as well as the four dollar generic.

150
00:16:16,780 --> 00:16:22,180
PhRMA, the pharmaceutical lobby, has consistently opposed comparative effectiveness research.

151
00:16:22,860 --> 00:16:31,000
When Congress created P.C.O.R.I. (the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute) in twenty-ten, industry successfully

152
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:36,440
lobbied to prohibit it from making coverage recommendations based on cost-effectiveness.

153
00:16:37,840 --> 00:16:43,520
Congress literally passed a law saying "don't fund research that might lower drug prices."

154
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:49,760
They wrote it down. In legislation. On purpose. With their names on it.

155
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:57,120
And then they went home and told their constituents they were fighting for affordable healthcare, because your species'

156
00:16:57,380 --> 00:17:04,660
capacity for doublethink is genuinely Olympian. This pattern held during the deadliest pandemic in a century.

157
00:17:05,339 --> 00:17:12,339
Operation Warp Speed initially selected fourteen vaccine candidates and planned head-to-head comparative studies in primates.

158
00:17:12,859 --> 00:17:20,099
Then dropped the comparative studies without explanation. Backed five companies with billions, gave others nothing.

159
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:27,480
The selection process was opaque enough to draw Senate scrutiny; when Senator Tammy Baldwin asked the Biomedical Advanced

160
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:35,260
Research and Development Authority, or BARDA's acting director to name the companies receiving public funds, he refused,

161
00:17:35,340 --> 00:17:41,480
citing "procurement sensitivity". No published cost-benefit analysis for excluding any candidate.

162
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:49,340
The W.H.O. ran a Solidarity Trial for Vaccines doing exactly this kind of head-to-head comparison.

163
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:59,060
The U.S. didn't join. Notice the pattern: the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute, or PCORI is banned from comparing

164
00:17:59,060 --> 00:18:07,620
drug cost-effectiveness. Researching COVID to Enhance Recovery, or RECOVER ignores the treatments patients actually want.

165
00:18:08,820 --> 00:18:16,840
O.W.S. drops the comparative monkey studies. Every manufacturer has financial interests; Pfizer had them, Moderna had them,

166
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:24,700
and that wasn't disqualifying. "Financial interest" only becomes a reason not to test when the treatment is cheap, generic,

167
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:31,120
or threatens incumbent revenue. The system tests what's profitable and ignores what isn't.

168
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:36,160
The revolving door helps. N.I.H. advisory committees include industry representatives.

169
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:42,780
Former N.I.H. officials join pharmaceutical boards. Academic researchers depend on industry grants.

170
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:50,380
Everyone's incentives align against cheap, fast trials testing whether expensive drugs outperform generics.

171
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:55,770
It's not a conspiracy. Conspiracies require secrecy. This is all public.

172
00:18:56,280 --> 00:19:04,060
It's just that everyone involved happens to benefit from the same terrible outcome, purely by coincidence, repeatedly, for

173
00:19:04,060 --> 00:19:09,780
decades. On Wishonia, we have a word for this. The word is "conspiracy."

174
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:15,380
The difference between a conspiracy and a system is whether the participants need to meet in a dark room.

175
00:19:15,860 --> 00:19:20,260
These people meet in conference rooms with catering. The outcome is identical.

176
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:24,780
The three point three percent allocation isn't a mystery. It's a business model.

177
00:19:25,540 --> 00:19:29,220
Basic research produces publications. Publications produce tenure.

178
00:19:29,580 --> 00:19:34,040
Tenure produces more grants. Individual researchers want cures.

179
00:19:34,080 --> 00:19:37,760
The system rewards them for everything except producing one.

180
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:45,520
Actual treatments are a byproduct that occasionally happens when someone forgets to optimize for papers and accidentally

181
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:53,100
optimizes for results instead. It's like a restaurant where the chefs are passionate about food but get promoted based on

182
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:57,710
how many menus they print. The menus are exquisite. They have won awards.

183
00:19:58,420 --> 00:20:02,280
The diners are eating the menus. Nobody has mentioned this.

184
00:20:02,660 --> 00:20:08,940
You Pay Twice. The N.I.H. claims to produce "public goods" like open science and training.

185
00:20:09,360 --> 00:20:16,020
What it actually produces is a free R. and D. department for pharmaceutical companies, funded by the same people who then

186
00:20:16,020 --> 00:20:24,600
pay full price for the drugs. Taxpayers funded research behind ninety-nine percent of new drugs (three hundred and fifty-six

187
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:29,800
drugs, twenty-ten to twenty-nineteen). Industry patents the winners.

188
00:20:30,340 --> 00:20:37,060
The public pays again through high drug prices. When research leads to a dead end, the public eats the loss.

189
00:20:37,640 --> 00:20:43,700
You pay for the lottery ticket. They cash the winnings. You'd think someone would have mentioned this arrangement before

190
00:20:43,700 --> 00:20:52,000
now, and someone has, many times, but the people who cash the winnings also fund the campaigns of the people who could change

191
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:57,560
the arrangement, so the arrangement continues. The only things the N.I.H.

192
00:20:57,560 --> 00:21:06,040
*should* fund are things private companies *cannot* profitably fund: open pragmatic trial platforms, repurposing of generic

193
00:21:06,040 --> 00:21:14,460
drugs where no patent means no profit means no interest, and comparative effectiveness data from head-to-head trials revealing

194
00:21:14,460 --> 00:21:20,180
which drugs actually work best (pharma's nightmare, your potential salvation).

195
00:21:20,760 --> 00:21:26,020
Instead, the N.I.H. explicitly avoids them to "not crowd out industry."

196
00:21:26,380 --> 00:21:33,600
Translation: "We won't run the trials that would lower drug prices, because the people who set drug prices asked us nicely."

197
